; M3 gate: PITCH-BAKED samples and the mixer loop that has no resampler
; (driver.md §14.2, mmb.md §10).
;
; The exporter resamples every UNLOOPED sample to the rate that makes it advance
; exactly one byte a DAC tick at the note it is played at, one blob per note,
; deduplicated by content. The sequencer then takes that voice's increment as a
; constant instead of computing it, and ms_bind puts the voice on the loop copy
; with `inc de` where the 16.16 resampler used to be — 37 Z80 cycles a tick.
;
; Measured on a score that overruns (two voices, dense shots, shift 4):
;   before  63,041 cyc (106%)  music x256 00cb (79%)  lost 12/s
;   after   59,376 cyc ( 99%)  music x256 00f0 (94%)  lost  4/s
; Inside the budget the two are indistinguishable, and that is not a smaller
; version of the same thing: Timer B paces the frame, so a cheaper mixer waits
; longer at the gate. The saving is collectable only where the frame is
; work-bound.
;
; Both paths run in the same frame here, which is the point of the gate:
;   pcm1  one-shot blip at three notes -> three baked entries, no resampler
;   pcm2  a LOOPED pad -> never baked (resampling moves the loop points off
;         integer samples, and rounding them back detunes the sustain), so it
;         stays on the 16.16 copies
(def blip :sample :file "blip.wav")
(def pad  :sample :file "pad.wav" :loop-start 100 :loop-end 400)
(pcm1 blip :tempo 120 :oct 4 :len 8 :vel 15   c e g c e g c e g c e g)
(pcm2 pad  :mode loop :tempo 120 :oct 4 :len 2 :vel 12   c _ c _ c _)
